    "confidence": "low",
    "plain": "Expanding the British Business Bank to crowd in private investment in zero-carbon products and technologies could help fund clean-tech businesses that struggle to access finance, but the actual green impact depends on how much of the expanded capacity is directed at climate investments rather than other sectors. SME reluctance to take on green investment finance is a real constraint.",
    "biggest_unknown": "Whether SME demand for green finance will be sufficient to absorb the supply, given that the share of SMEs planning steps to reduce their carbon footprint fell from 27% to 19% between 2021 and 2023.",
    "claims": [
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "Cost concerns are a major barrier: 57% of SMEs intending to reduce climate impact cited rising costs as making them reluctant to take on external finance.",
        "evidence_id": "E14",
        "quote": "by 57% of those intending to reduce their climate impact, making them reluctant to take on external finance for sustainability improvements"
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "The expanded BBB role is expected to accelerate green investment by absorbing risk and stimulating further private capital.",
        "evidence_id": "E12",
        "quote": "The expanded role is expected to accelerate green investment by absorbing risk and stimulating further private capital in this area"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "Clean energy industries are listed as one of eight priority growth sectors for BBB strategic targeting.",
        "evidence_id": "E5",
        "quote": "clean energy industries"
      }
    ],
    "rationale": "The policy commits the BBB to actively crowd in private investment in zero-carbon products and technologies, addressing a real and evidenced funding gap of an estimated \u00a36.8 billion annually for SME green investment. The BBB's existing cleantech track record (\u00a3351m across 89 companies) and its historical leverage ratio of over \u00a34 of private investment per \u00a31 of public funds suggest the mechanism is credible. Clean energy is also named as a priority sector, adding some structural weight to the green commitment.\n\nHowever, there are important constraints. First, cleantech has historically represented only 3% of BBB equity investment \u2014 the policy does not commit a specific green allocation from the expanded capacity, so the zero-carbon element competes with seven other priority sectors. Second, the demand-side is weakening: fewer SMEs are planning green investments, and cost pressures deter even those that want to act. Supply-side finance without demand is unlikely to deliver material emissions or nature outcomes at population scale. Third, the crowding-in projections (\u00a326bn private capital) come from the BBB itself and should be treated as optimistic projected-tier estimates.\n\nThe net effect on O6 is a modest, long-term improvement in the financing environment for clean-tech SMEs \u2014 real but not transformative. The absence of ring-fenced green capital, weakening SME green appetite, and the dominance of non-green sectors in the BBB's current portfolio limit the magnitude. Confidence is low because the decisive parameter \u2014 what share of expanded BBB activity is genuinely additional green investment \u2014 is unspecified in the policy.",
    "time_split": {
      "near_direction": "negligible",
      "long_direction": "improves",
      "note": "Institutional capacity builds gradually; green investment effects on emissions and nature accrue over years, not within a parliament."
    },
